      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "If your framerate does not change no matter what resolution you set then you are either at the framerate limit for your screen or you are CPU bottlenecked.\nIf you are talking about desktop, then you can choose to increase the limit by adding --fps=number to your launch parameters. Though it should match your monitor so if you expect your monitor to be 120 or something you should probably double check that you have the correct settings there first.\nIf you are talking about VR, then it is recommended to run at the same refresh rate as your headset. Some headsets allow you to change the refresh rate, and you should research what options you have there.\nIf your issue is that your framerate is wavering around 60 and above rather than locked at 60, then it likely has nothing to do with refresh rate and is instead that you are CPU limited.\nIt is very common to be CPU bottlenecked in VRChat when you are in an instance with many people or in a world with expensive udon scripts. If you are getting this performance in the VRChat home world when alone I would worry, but at a large event with 40+ people, 60 fps actually sounds pretty good! Ask around and see what other people are getting and you may be above average, if anything. If you are getting unusual performance compared to other people, you should look at the CPU instead of the GPU.\nNote that CPU usage % doesn't really correlate to performance at all. If you want a more accurate tool to check performance you should get FPSVR.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
